10. Security & Privacy

Risk Mitigation
API key leakage via XSS Encrypt keys at rest with Web Crypto AES-GCM; never render keys in DOM; use CSP headers via meta tag; sanitize all user-rendered markdown
API key exfiltration to Hedetangen servers No backend exists. The app makes direct fetch calls to provider endpoints only. Document this clearly in the UI.
Data loss on browser clear Provide export functionality; warn users that clearing site data deletes their studio.
Prompt injection via web content If web-search results are incorporated, treat them as untrusted and delimit them clearly in prompts. Jennifer’s system prompt instructs her to ignore instructions embedded in retrieved content.
Hallucinated brand assets Brand DNA honesty rule injected into every expert prompt; Jennifer performs a final guardrail check before returning output.

Privacy notice (plain language, in settings):

"Hedetangen AI Studio runs entirely in your browser. Your API key and your content are stored on this device only. When you chat, your message is sent directly to [Provider] using your key. Nothing is sent to Hedetangen or any other server. Export your data regularly if you want a backup."

---

11. Risks & Mitigations

Risk Impact Mitigation
Free-tier rate limits frustrate users High Display quota metadata; Jennifer suggests alternative models; exponential backoff with clear UI feedback
Free model quality varies Medium Recommend llama-3.3-70b-versatile or gpt-oss-120b for copy; show model capability notes
Brand DNA ignored by weaker models High Inject guardrails at the top of every system prompt; Jennifer explicitly verifies outputs; user can enable "Strict mode" that rejects outputs mentioning structures without a reference
User loses API key or data Medium Export reminder after first week of use; prominent backup button in settings
OpenRouter free model list changes frequently Low Fetch on demand; show "last refreshed" timestamp; allow manual model ID entry as a fallback
Single-page app becomes hard to maintain Medium Modular ES modules; no framework lock-in; clear provider abstraction

